  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe adopt a hybridized optimization framework combining a structured initial pattern with a multi-phase evolutionary search, followed by a local refinement phase. The core idea is to leverage the non-convex nature of the problem by exploring diverse initial configurations and applying a multi-objective evolutionary algorithm with tailored operators to escape local optima. This method addresses the limitations of gradient-based approaches by using evolutionary diversity preservation and adaptive mutation strategies. The key bottleneck of previous approaches is their reliance on fixed solvers without explicit control over solution diversity and constraint adherence. By using a structured initial pattern based on low-autocorrelation sequence principles, we aim to reduce the initial overlap, allowing local optimization to refine the solution effectively.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points with \u2211h = n_points / 2\n- Objective: minimize c5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max_k (dx * corr_k) where corr_k = \u2211h[i](1-h[i+k]) for valid i+k\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n\nImplementation:\n- Use exact correlation computation with proper scaling (dx = 2/n_points)\n- Employ continuous representation with Gaussian smoothing for smoothness\n- Maintain dual representations (discrete and continuous) for validation\n- Use exact objective evaluation without approximations or surrogates\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation\n- Check if initial_h_values is valid (sum \u2248 n_points/2, entries \u2208 [0,1])\n- If provided, use as base with 5% random perturbation\n- Otherwise, generate a base pattern using a low-autocorrelation seed:\n  - Generate a binary sequence with low autocorrelation (using a Turyn sequence)\n  - Rescale to [0,1] and interpolate to continuous function\n  - Ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2 by final adjustment\n\nStage 2: Initialization\n- Generate 5 distinct initial patterns:\n  1. Base pattern from Stage 1\n  2. Random binary vector with \u2211h = n_points/2\n  3. Optimized LP relaxation (constrained h[i] = 0.5 + \u03b5*sin(\u03c0i/n_points))\n  4. Structured pattern with 10% density of 1s and 1s at symmetric points\n  5. Pattern generated by spacing 1s with Fibonacci sequence spacing\n\nStage 3: Core optimization\n- Initialize a genetic algorithm (DEAP library) with:\n  - Population size: 50\n  - Selection: Tournament (size 5)\n  - Crossover: Blend (alpha=0.5)\n  - Mutation: Polynomial (eta=0.5, indpb=0.1)\n  - Termination: 500 generations or stagnation\n  - Fitness function: c5 value (lower is better)\n  - Constraint handling: penalty term for sum deviation (weight=100)\n- Apply post-processing refinement:\n  - Use SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and equality constraint \u2211h = n_points/2\n  - Add penalty term for variability: \u2211|h[i] - h[i-1]| \u2264 0.5\n  - Early stopping if objective improves by <1e-5 over 50 iterations\n  - Apply feasibility repair: clip values and adjust sum with linear interpolation\n\nStage 4: Global search\n- For top 3 candidates from Stage 3:\n  1. Apply differential evolution with:\n     - Population size 100\n     - Mutation factor 0.8\n     - Crossover probability 0.9\n     - Hybridize with local optimization post-DE\n  2. Use memory-based selection to retain diverse candidates\n  3. Maintain diversity through mutation and elitism\n\nStage 5: Final evaluation\n- All candidates are re-evaluated with exact correlation computation\n- Select the candidate with smallest c5 value\n- Verify constraints: \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 (tolerance 1e-6), entries \u2208 [0,1]\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400 (balances resolution and computation time)\n- Initial patterns: 5 variations as specified\n- DE parameters: population size 100, mutation factor 0.8, crossover probability 0.9\n- Regularization strength: 0.5 for Lipschitz constraint\n- Fallback: Use initial_h_values if no better candidate found\n\nVariations:\n- When initial_h_values is available: \n  - Use it as base with 3% random perturbation\n  - Increase DE population to 150 for better exploration\n- When n_points < 300: \n  - Reduce DE iterations to 150\n  - Use simpler regularizer (0.3 Lipschitz)\n- When c5 < 0.38: \n  - Increase resolution to 600 points\n  - Add penalty for high variance (\u03c3^2(h) \u2264 0.1)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Stage 1: 10s (validation and initial pattern generation)\n- Stage 2: 30s (initial pattern creation)\n- Stage 3: 200s (core optimization)\n- Stage 4: 200s (global search)\n- Stage 5: 30s (final evaluation)\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Time monitoring: Check elapsed time every 30s during optimization\n- Early stopping: Terminate optimization if no improvement in 100 iterations\n- Fallback: If time runs out, return the best candidate found\n- Numerical stability: Clip all values between 0 and 1 before final evaluation\n- Reconstruction: For new n_points, resample the best pattern with linear interpolation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All returned h_values must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 within 1e-6 tolerance\n- No NaN or infinite values in the returned array\n\nValidation procedure:\n1. Recompute \u2211h and verify against n_points/2\n2. Check all entries are within [0,1]\n3. Recompute the correlation objective with exact dx = 2/n_points\n4. Verify that the returned c5_bound matches the computed value\n\nSanity checks:\n- Check that \u2211h is exactly n_points/2 (within 1e-9)\n- Verify that the correlation array has length 2*n_points - 1\n- Confirm that the maximum value in the correlation array is properly scaled\n- Ensure that the returned h_values are not a copy of initial_h_values",
